Imagine code like this:
```rust
const fn less_than<const A: usize, const B: usize>() -> bool { A < B }

fn foo<const N: usize>()
    where ???
{
    let a: [u8; {2*N}] = ..;
    let b: [u8; {4*N}] = ..;
}
```
Naive bubbling will result in two conditions `less_than(N, usize::MAX/2)` and `less_than(N, usize::MAX/4)`. We can immediately see that the first condition is redundant, but it's not so easy to prove it in a general case.

But even before that, how compiler will be able to tell that `less_than(N, usize::MAX/2)` is enough to guarantee that `2*N` will not overflow? We have roughly two approaches:
- Bake into compiler type-level operators like `>`, `<`, `=`, `+`, `*`, etc., special case handling of numeric types on const level, and build limited reasoning capabilities around all of it.
- Let `less_than` be a user-defined `const fn`, and make compiler smart enough, so it will be able to deduce necessary properties from analyzing the const function, which in turn will be used for proving that const computations inside a function complete without issues.

The first option will be very limiting and special-casing some types and operators certainly does not look great. But the second option is essentially a limited formal verification framework, which will be really hard to pull off.

