I am coming from an hobbyist C and Rust background, not C++, but as I understand, that is invalid in both C and C++, for different reasons.

For C, it's trivial: the `enum class` declaration is not valid, as it has no concept of classes. The [RFC text](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/blob/master/text/2195-really-tagged-unions.md#guide-level-explanation) suggests just using the backing integer type, but, as of C23, simply omitting the word `class` should make this valid in C, though adoption of new C versions is quite slow.

As I understand it, C++ only allows the most recently-set union variant to be accessed, so even though the tag is at the same offset regardless of variant, it is undefined behavior to read it unless, by some miracle of statistics, it always happens to go through the correct variant.

Because splitting the tag and the payload into separate members of a struct could cause alignment issues, I don't believe there's any way to rewrite this to be valid, standards-compliant C++ for all possible `#[repr(C)]` enums.
